# Robot code conventions

## Toolchain — 2027 overrides that displace older training data

**Read this section before generating any WPILib code. It contradicts most WPILib material that
exists, and the older material is what a model has seen most of.**

WPILib 2027 uses the Java package root **`org.wpilib`**. It **replaces `edu.wpi.first`, which no
longer exists.** Both halves matter: naming only the new root leaves the old one in place as a
default. When an example, tutorial, or forum answer targets a release before 2027, do not carry its
package root forward.

**Do not generate these identifiers.** They are removed, not deprecated — removed code does not
compile at all, where deprecated code compiles with a warning:

- any import beginning `edu.wpi.first.`
- `edu.wpi.first.wpilibj.XboxController` / `PS4Controller` / `PS5Controller` / `StadiaController` —
  2027 replaces all four, and **which class replaces them depends on the Driver Station**. On the
  2027 FIRST Driver Station the documented replacement is `org.wpilib.driverstation.Gamepad`. On the
  NI Driver Station the documented Xbox replacement is
  `org.wpilib.driverstation.NiDsXboxController`; replacements for PS4, PS5 and Stadia were **not
  established** — do not infer them from parallel class names
- `edu.wpi.first.wpilibj2.command.*` — 2027 uses `org.wpilib.command2.*`
- `CommandXboxController` — its 2027 replacement was **not established**; do not infer one from
  `CommandGamepad` or `CommandNiDsXboxController`

The controller classes changed **twice over**: the package moved *and* the class is a different
class. Fixing only the import path still leaves an unresolved symbol. **Ask which Driver Station
this team runs before choosing one; do not pick one for them.**

`edu.wpi.first.math.*` is not a root-only rewrite either — some sub-packages moved as well:
`edu.wpi.first.math.Matrix` is `org.wpilib.math.linalg.Matrix`, `edu.wpi.first.math.MathUtil` is
`org.wpilib.math.util.MathUtil`, `edu.wpi.first.math.Nat` is `org.wpilib.math.util.Nat`, and
`edu.wpi.first.math.system.plant.DCMotor` is `org.wpilib.math.system.DCMotor`. The skill's
`references/namespaces.md` records every move it measured, with the method and that method's limits.

**Two command frameworks ship side by side** — `org.wpilib.command2` and `org.wpilib.command3`. They
are different models, and `Command` exists in both as different types. **Ask which one this project
uses; do not infer it.**

**Nothing in this baseline reads which WPILib version this project is on, and this file is
installed whether or not a robot project exists yet.** This section was written against a WPILib
2027 alpha, whose shipped release and development branch disagree today. Establish from the
project itself what it is actually on; if it is not on that alpha, or you cannot establish what
it is on, say so rather than generating code against this section.
When a class location matters and you cannot establish which the team has, **say so and give both**.
Do not pick one. The authoritative record of every fact in this section, with the ref each was
verified against, is the `current-wpilib-2027` skill — when this section and that skill disagree,
**the skill is right and this file is stale.**

## Build and run

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| Build | `./gradlew build` |
| Test | `./gradlew test` |
| Simulate (Java) | `./gradlew run` — see the note below |
| Add a vendor library | `./gradlew vendordep --url=<url>` |

**Simulation: use `run`, not `simulateJava`.** Measured against WPILib `2027.0.0-alpha-7`: there is
no `simulateJava` task. Gradle will abbreviate that name to `simulateExternalJava`, which **exits
without starting a robot program** — so `./gradlew simulateJava` can report success having
simulated nothing. The official 2027 documentation still says `simulateJava`; the build does not.
If your WPILib version restores it, prefer whatever `./gradlew tasks --all` actually lists.

**Do not run any `deploy` task.** Nothing in this baseline enforces that — see the safety section
below for what deploying safely actually requires of you.

The permission rules installed with this baseline pre-approve the first three of those commands as
the bare task forms written above. Append an option or an argument to one and it stops matching that
grant, so what happens next is whatever your permission mode does with a command no rule matched —
in the default mode, it asks you. That is expected, not a fault.

**Unless the longer form contains `deploy`, which is denied outright.** The deny rules are consulted
independently of the grants and win over them, and two of them match `deploy` anywhere in the
command — so `./gradlew build deploy` is denied, not merely unapproved. That is deliberate: a deploy
hidden behind another task name is exactly the shape those rules exist to stop.

**That rule is a speed bump, not a gate.** It matches command TEXT. A wrapper script, an alias, a
different working directory, or any spelling it does not match is not stopped. Nothing in this
baseline can prevent a deploy — see the safety section below.

**If this project already had a `.claude/settings.json` when the baseline was installed, none of
the rules described above were written** — the installer never modifies a settings file it did not
create. Check the file before relying on any of them.

Those permission rules need **Claude Code v2.1.228 or later**. Run `claude --version` and check.
Below that version the vendor's own permissions documentation does not establish that a write to a
path this baseline protects is checked at all, so protections you can see in the settings file may
not be in effect.

Never edit `vendordeps/`, `gradle/`, `gradlew`, `gradlew.bat`, or `.wpilib/`. Those belong to the
toolchain; editing them turns every upgrade into a merge conflict.

