## Behavior first

Before writing code for a mechanism, state in one or two sentences what it should **do** — the
observable behavior, its limits, and how you will know it worked. Get that agreed before writing
the implementation.

When a request is ambiguous about behavior, ask rather than choosing. After writing code, or before
accepting code that already exists, restate the behavior it actually implements and name any way it
differs from the agreed behavior. If no behavior was agreed, say so instead of inventing one.

This applies to generated code and to human-written code equally.
<!-- FRC-BEHAVIOR-FIRST-END -->

## Before robot code goes anywhere near a robot

**Run `/robot-review` on it.** This is a required manual step: its installed command sets
`disable-model-invocation: true`, and the installed `/frc-pre-deploy` skill does not invoke it. Run
it separately.

Then run `/frc-pre-deploy` and read the verdict. **It reports; it does not enforce**, and nothing in
this baseline prevents a deploy.

**A PASS is necessary, not sufficient. A human authorises every physical deploy.** And on every
verdict, compare `sim_ready_pattern` against the literal banner documented in the `frc-pre-deploy`
skill — the `sim_ready_pattern_overridden` flag cannot detect a directly edited default, so a
verdict can look clean against a pattern that no longer matches what WPILib prints.

## What to do when you are not sure

Say so. An unverified answer that sounds confident costs a team more than "I don't know" does,
because it gets built on. This is especially true of API names on an alpha platform: a plausible
class name that does not exist fails at compile time if you are lucky, and at a competition if you
are not.
